+            "content": "# Architecture Documentation: `archive_path` Module\n\n## 1. Component Overview\n\nThe `archive_path` module provides a single utility function, `archive_path`, designed to securely construct file paths within an archive extraction context. Its primary responsibility is to prevent **Path Traversal attacks** (also known as Zip Slip vulnerabilities) by validating that a given archive member name does not escape the intended extraction root directory.\n\nThis component acts as a security boundary between untrusted archive metadata (member names) and the filesystem. It ensures that any path derived from archive contents remains strictly contained within the specified `root` directory.\n\n## 2. Design Decisions\n\n### Security-First Path Construction\nThe core design decision is to treat archive member names as **untrusted input**. The function explicitly rejects any member name that:\n- Is an absolute path (e.g., `/etc/passwd`).\n- Contains path traversal sequences (`..` in any component).\n\nThis approach prioritizes safety over flexibility, which is appropriate for archive extraction scenarios where malicious or corrupted archives may attempt to write files outside the intended directory.\n\n### Use of `PurePosixPath`\nThe implementation uses `PurePosixPath` rather than `Path` for several reasons:\n- **Platform Independence**: `PurePosixPath` interprets path separators according to POSIX rules (`/`), regardless of the operating system. This is critical because archive formats (ZIP, TAR, etc.) store paths using POSIX conventions internally.\n- **No Filesystem Interaction**: `PurePosixPath` performs purely syntactic operations without checking for the existence of files or directories on the actual filesystem. This makes the function fast, side-effect-free, and deterministic—ideal for a validation step that may be called thousands of times during extraction.\n- **Consistent Behavior**: Using a \"pure\" path class ensures that the validation logic behaves identically on Windows, Linux, and macOS, even though the underlying OS path separator may differ.\n\n### Fail-Fast Exception Strategy\nInstead of silently sanitizing unsafe paths (which could lead to unexpected file locations), the function raises a `ValueError` immediately. This makes misuse or malicious input explicit and forces the caller to handle the error, preventing silent data corruption or security bypasses.\n\n## 3. Component Diagram\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    A[Caller / Extraction Engine] -->|root, member| B(archive_path function)\n    B --> C{Path Validation}\n    C -->|Absolute path?| D[Reject: ValueError]\n    C -->|Contains '..'?| D\n    C -->|Safe path| E[Path Joining]\n    E --> F[Return: PurePosixPath]\n    D --> G[Error Handling Layer]\n    \n    style D fill:#f99,stroke:#333\n    style F fill:#9f9,stroke:#333\n    style B fill:#eee,stroke:#333\n```\n\n## 4. Interfaces\n\n### Function Signature\n\n```python\ndef archive_path(root: PurePosixPath, member: str) -> PurePosixPath\n```\n\n| Parameter | Type | Description |\n|-----------|------|-------------|\n| `root` | `PurePosixPath` | The base directory within which all archive members will be extracted. |\n| `member` | `str` | The relative path of the member as stored in the archive (untrusted input). |\n\n### Return Value\n\n- **Success**: Returns a `PurePosixPath` representing the safe, fully-resolved path within `root`.\n- **Failure**: Raises `ValueError(\"unsafe member\")` if the `member` path is absolute or contains traversal sequences.\n\n### Integration Points\n\n- **Archive Extraction Engines**: This function should be called for every member before any file I/O operation occurs during archive extraction.\n- **Filesystem Layer**: The returned `PurePosixPath` is consumed by the filesystem abstraction layer to perform actual file writes.\n\n## 5. Data Flow\n\n1. **Input**: The caller provides a `root` path (the extraction destination) and a `member` name (from archive metadata).\n2. **Normalization**: The `member` string is wrapped in a `PurePosixPath` object to enable syntactic path analysis.\n3. **Validation**:\n   - Check if the path is absolute (`name.is_absolute()`).\n   - Check if any component of the path is `..` (`\"..\" in name.parts`).\n   - If either check fails, raise `ValueError`.\n4. **Path Joining**: If validation passes, the `member` path is joined with the `root` path using the `/` operator, producing a new `PurePosixPath`.\n5. **Output**: The resulting path is returned to the caller, which uses it to determine the final file location for extraction.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Caller\n    participant archive_path\n    participant Filesystem\n\n    Caller->>archive_path: root=\"/extract\", member=\"safe/file.txt\"\n    archive_path->>archive_path: Validate member\n    archive_path-->>Caller: PurePosixPath(\"/extract/safe/file.txt\")\n    Caller->>Filesystem: Write to /extract/safe/file.txt\n\n    Caller->>archive_path: root=\"/extract\", member=\"../../etc/passwd\"\n    archive_path->>archive_path: Validate member\n    archive_path--xCaller: ValueError(\"unsafe member\")\n    Note over Caller: Handle error / skip member\n```\n\n## 6. Extension Points\n\n### Custom Validation Rules\nThe current implementation only checks for absolute paths and `..` traversal. To extend security, callers can add additional validation before or after calling `archive_path`:\n- **Symlink Check**: If the archive may contain symlinks, add logic to reject members that are symlinks (though this requires filesystem awareness, which `PurePosixPath` does not provide).\n- **Whitelist/Blacklist**: Pre-filter member names against a list of allowed or forbidden prefixes.\n- **Length Limits**: Reject paths that exceed filesystem length limits.\n\n### Platform-Specific Adaptation\nWhile `PurePosixPath` is used for consistency, if the target system requires Windows-style paths or has different security policies, the function can be wrapped