+            "content": "# `split_records` Module Documentation\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `split_records` module provides a single utility function, `split_records`, designed to parse a string into a list of clean, non-empty records. Its primary purpose is to convert delimited text (where records are separated by a configurable delimiter) into a structured Python list, while simultaneously removing extraneous whitespace and filtering out empty entries.\n\nThis is a common pattern in data processing pipelines where raw input data might contain inconsistent spacing around delimiters or trailing separators that result in empty strings.\n\n## Key Concepts\n\n### Delimiter-Based Parsing\nThe function relies on Python's built-in `str.split()` method, which partitions a string at each occurrence of a specified separator character or substring. This makes the function highly flexible for different data formats (CSV-like, pipe-delimited, tab-delimited, etc.).\n\n### Data Cleaning via Comprehension\nRather than performing split and cleaning in separate steps, the function uses a list comprehension to combine three operations in a single pass:\n1. **Splitting**: Dividing the input string by the separator.\n2. **Stripping**: Removing leading and trailing whitespace from each part.\n3. **Filtering**: Excluding any parts that are empty (or whitespace-only) after stripping.\n\n### Default Configuration\nThe separator defaults to `\"|\"` (pipe), suggesting this function was likely designed with a specific use case in mind—possibly parsing pipe-delimited values from logs, configuration files, or simple data exports.\n\n## How It Works\n\nThe `split_records` function operates in three logical stages:\n\n### 1. Input Validation\n```python\nif not separator:\n    raise ValueError(\"separator must not be empty\")\n```\nThe function first checks if the `separator` argument is empty. This guards against calling `text.split(\"\")`, which would raise a `ValueError` in Python with a less descriptive message. By raising a custom `ValueError` here, the function provides clearer feedback to the caller.\n\n### 2. String Splitting\n```python\ntext.split(separator)\n```\nThe input `text` is split into a list of substrings at each occurrence of the `separator`. For example, `\"a|b|c\"` with separator `\"|\"` produces `[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]`.\n\n### 3. Cleaning and Filtering\n```python\n[part.strip() for part in ... if part.strip()]\n```\nEach substring from the split is:\n- **Stripped** of leading and trailing whitespace using `.strip()`.\n- **Filtered** to exclude any substring that becomes empty after stripping.\n\nThis two-use of `.strip()` is intentional:\n- The first `part.strip()` in the expression produces the cleaned value to be included in the result.\n- The second `part.strip()` in the `if` condition checks whether the cleaned value is non-empty. If the original part was `\" \"` (only spaces), `.strip()` returns `\"\"`, which is falsy, so it is excluded from the result.\n\n## Usage Examples\n\n### Basic Usage with Default Separator\n```python\n>>> split_records(\"foo|bar|baz\")\n['foo', 'bar', 'baz']\n```\n\n### Handling Whitespace Around Delimiters\n```python\n>>> split_records(\"foo | bar | baz\")\n['foo', 'bar', 'baz']\n```\nThe spaces around the pipe characters are removed by `.strip()`.\n\n### Filtering Empty Records\n```python\n>>> split_records(\"foo||bar|\")\n['foo', 'bar']\n```\nThe double pipe `||` produces an empty string between them, and the trailing pipe produces a final empty string. Both are filtered out.\n\n### Custom Separator\n```python\n>>> split_records(\"one,two,three\", separator=\",\")\n['one', 'two', 'three']\n\n>>> split_records(\"one;two;three\", separator=\";\")\n['one', 'two', 'three']\n```\n\n### Edge Cases\n```python\n>>> split_records(\"\")\n[]\n\n>>> split_records(\"   \")\n[]\n\n>>> split_records(\"|\")\n[]\n```\n\n### Error Case\n```python\n>>> split_records(\"a|b\", separator=\"\")\nValueError: separator must not be empty\n```\n\n## Integration\n\nNo directly related modules were identified in the codebase. The `split_records` function is a self-contained utility that can be imported and used independently:\n\n```python\nfrom module import split_records\n\nrecords = split_records(raw_input, separator=\"|\")\nfor record in records:\n    process(record)\n```\n\nBecause it has no dependencies on other parts of the system, it can be easily integrated into data ingestion scripts, log parsers, or any component that needs to normalize delimited text input into a clean list of values.",
